A||||12||TRiAD||Manual Trannies attatched to the 2.8 (4sp, 5sp, 5sp Isuzu)||||1||
Z||000000||TRiAD||11-20-2001||06:35 PM||||OK, I have an'85 GT with the 4sp manual.<P>I'm planning on autocrossing the car (if I ever get it back on the road) and was told the stock 4sp was "stiff". <BR>Mine won't even shift at all now, so I can't use my own experience with it, since it was most likely gone when I bought it. I have less than 350 miles on it since I bought it.<P>My question is this; I was told to use the Isuzu 5sp manual (from the dukes) since they apparently shift quickly and smoothly, similar to most Hondas. <BR>Can anyone give their driving impression of the Isuzu 5sp manual? Especially with the 2.8. Has anyone had trouble with the Isuzu 5sp manual with high HP and/or tourque applications? (3800SC/V6Turbo/V8).<P>Thanks!<BR><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://communities.msn.com/_Secure/0RAAAAN8VWdTblRDE93zkbJ*AbWuOFSwE0rtNS2h23KCe3aGgj1O!JDqud!IoFt1NPvdq6rh9ef*226xJh!nSf*vv2Id5gL8WoDCkDX*tCeM/1985-Signature-2.gif">    <BR><B><I><FONT COLOR="blue">Project MR1 ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</B> ~ Photoshop rendition of planned modifications</I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000001||TylerB11||11-20-2001||06:46 PM||||I also have an 85 GT - I'm looking to upgrade to a 5 speed as well. I find the 4-spd very stiff. Also Its killing my gas mileage. I haven't decided to go with the Isuzu or the Getrag, so many this thread will help..||||reg||1
Z||000002||Dan Robinson||11-20-2001||06:47 PM||||I'm about to hook up a Isuzu 5 up to a 2.8, but it's not goin to be done for a couple of months.  But I talked to someone who autocrossed with this setup and it was fine for him.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Raydar||11-20-2001||07:23 PM||||Never driven a V-6 with an Isuzu behind it, but have heard many people say the gear ratios are much better than the Getrag. <BR>I've also heard that the 4 speed V-6 is actually quicker than than the Getrag. You might want to take this into account before you spend a bunch of money on a 5 speed.<BR>As for the durability of the Isuzu, Archie puts them behind (beside?) V-8s on a regular basis. The weakest part of the Isuzu is the shift fork. Supposed to be made of pot metal or some such. They should be fine if you don't speed shift them. <BR>The only manual V-6 I've ever owned was an 85 4-speed GT. It was a little notchy, but not terrible. <BR>Just my $.02.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 11-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||jelly2m8||11-20-2001||08:11 PM|||| I have tried all the 2.8 / manual transaxle combinations.<P> Here is MY input based on personal experiances with stock 2.8's.<P> The most fun is the 3.65 4spd, only it lacks that 5th gear for hi-speed crusing.( 90 mph+)<P> The 4.10 4 spd pulls like hell, it suffers from a good top speed, and takes away from gas mileage. The engine will turn at 3000 rpm at 60 mph, with stock 215/60R14' tires.<P> The Getrag suits me best,I do alot of spirited driving , not as quick as the 4 spds ( excuding the 3.32) Good all around performance, best top end performance and 35 mpg+ to boot.<P> The Isuzu only shines over 65 mph, that won't be of much use in auto-xing. Lacks in acceleration from the previous 3, good gas mileage.<P> The 3.32 sits in the same class as the isuzu, great high way tranny.<P> I am not very impressed with the acceleration properties of the isuzu / 2.8 combo, I think the 3.65 4 spd is going in that car next spring.<P> My opinion is to stick with the 3.65 4spd, if you are using this car as a auto-x'r, and a DD.<BR> Go with the 4.10 4spd if it is going to be a solely auto-x'r<P> All the Fiero manual transaxles are pretty reliable, you have to abuse them heavily to have failures.<P> The 4 spds are a bit notchier shifting than the Isuzu, and getrag, but then again, if yer auto-xing the car, you'll be grabing gears anyhow. <BR>Tip on shifting the 4 spd, when you shift, make like you want to rip that shifter out of the console.<P><BR>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by jelly2m8 (edited 11-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||SCCA FIERO||11-20-2001||09:09 PM||||I owned an 86 SE V6 4 speed for a number of years. The thing pulled hard, but shifting was horrible and the clutch required me to almost stand on it to get it to the floor (joking, a little).<P>Now I own an 88 Coupe with the same engine from that SE.... But it has an Isuzu 5spd in it. I gotta tell you that thing shifts like a hot knife through warm butter! The clutch pedal will fall to the floor if you look at it hard enough (joking, a little). Not to mention reverse is where it's supposed to be, and my reverse lights work now. That lever in the shifter for the reverse lights never worked unless I held the shifter to the left.<P>I autocrossed both cars... the 86 with the 4spd needed to shift into 3rd at times. Second was too "revvy". Touch the gas at the wrong time and you're "Cone King" for that event. Shift to 3rd and the engine would bog. The Isuzu's second gear works great through the entire couse usually. 1st gear (3.73) is used sometimes for those real tight 180 degree turns that require you to almost stop.<P>Anyway, I prefer the Isuzu. If you care at all  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">.<P>------------------<BR>-Chris <BR>88 Fiero Coupe (V6 & fastback conversion)<BR><B>You must be fast, cuz I was haulin ass when I passed you!</B>||||reg||1
Z||000006||TylerB11||11-20-2001||09:18 PM||||So for a 2.8 used as a total DD ( possible turbo in the future) you guys recommend the getrag? I like the aceleration of the 4.10 4 spd I have, but being at such high RPMs on the highway is slaughtering my gas mileage, especially with the small tank, not much driving range.||||reg||1
Z||000007||TRiAD||11-20-2001||10:33 PM||||Thanks all! I'm looking for <I>easy</I> shifts, and the mileage wouldn't hurt, either...<P>I'm planning on the Isuzu so far...<P>Any others?||||reg||1
Z||000008||Cooter||11-20-2001||10:39 PM||||I have the 4.10's and IF I ever change the tranny it will be an Isuzu. Not so much for the ultimate performance, but economy- both gas mileage and cheaper to replace if broken.||||reg||1
Z||000009||TRiAD||11-21-2001||11:09 AM||||Does $1500 sound fair to everyone for an Isuzu swap, with new clutch and clutch cylinders?<P>Also, has anyone had Twin Lakes do this for them? How are they on this,and how long did it take? (I'm still waiting on a clutch pedal that apparently has yet to be removed from the donor...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> )<p>[This message has been edited by TRiAD (edited 11-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||SCCA FIERO||11-21-2001||09:10 PM||||That's an aweful lot of money for that swap. You have the 4 speed, right? Well, all you need to do is swap trannies, shifters, cables, clutches, TO bearing and shed a little skin from your knuckles. Can you not do this swap yourself? No place to do it or tools? I hope you aren't affraid to do it youreself because you can do this swap for around 300 bucks (providing you have a few tools) and around 3 days at the most. I bought an extra Isuzu tranny with shifter, cables and a bunch of 88 suspension and brake parts for 150 bucks.<P>If you lived close enough, I would help you from start to finish. Find a friend to help ya. Most of all, when you're done, you can say, "I did it."||||reg||1
Z||000011||TRiAD||11-21-2001||11:04 PM||||No, I really don't have any way t do it, and for the $1500 he was going to use a new clutch and replace the clutch cylinders as well. <P>For the way things are now, I'm not sure when this car will ever see pavement again. I don't have the money to fix it. I sold a car that worked fine to buy this, and now I'm out a car. <P>If anyone sees this, and has tips on how it could maybe be done, or is available to help, etc, let me know. <P>Thanks. ||||reg||1
Z||000012||FieroMaster88||11-22-2001||01:45 AM||||I love my Isuzu setup!  It works awesome.  I can beat a friend of mine.  He as a 87GT with a 5 speed Getrag.  Don't beat him by much....maybe half a car length..but I beat him.  shifts smooth and accelerates nicely!<P>$1500 does sound kinda high to me tho.  I paid $140 for a new clutch and about $100 for a new clutch master and slave cylinder.  Plus another $30 for a steel clutch petal.  I got the trans for free from a friends parts car though, but you should be able to find the setup cheaper than $1500.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jamessig.jpg">                   <BR>James Essar<BR>AOL IM Name: FieroMaster88<BR>Not your ordinary 88 coupe!||||reg||1
